< Couplings of finite distributions carry two algebraic structures, and this note determines both. The first is the
< \emph{gluing algebra} $A_f$: matrices whose row and column sums are multiples of a distribution $f$, multiplied by
< the gluing lemma. We show that $A_f\cong\R\times M_{n-1}(\R)$, with the product coupling $f\otimes f$ and its
< complement $\diag(f)-f\otimes f$ as the two central idempotents. So the circulations form a direct factor, the
< mass functional is the projection onto the other factor, and the algebra sees nothing of positivity. Whiskering by a
< kernel is not multiplicative. Its defect makes it an $A_\infty$-functor into the square-zero cone of the circulations,
< and we show that this functor is the unique one with its first component, for every mass-preserving linear map, not
< only for whiskering. Up to homotopy it is the mass functional: the cone is quasi-isomorphic to $\R$, the gluing algebra
< is separable, and so every homotopy invariant of a functor that kills the circulations sees only the total mass. We
< also show that couplings with kernels as vertical arrows do not form a double category: squares compose horizontally
< for bijections, but not for some constant kernels or for some non-injective functions. Categorically, $A_f$ is the linear span of the
< monoid of $\D$-algebra endomorphisms of $\D(Y)$ that fix $f$, the product coupling is the endomorphism through the zero
< object of the category of probability spaces, and the mass is the map to that zero object; the context comonad of the
< tower paper is acted on by this monoid, but not linearly, so it does not see $A_f$. The second structure is the
< \emph{ring of contingency tables}: the Ehrhart ring of a coupling polytope with rational margins. It is generated in
< degree one, its Hilbert function counts integer tables with scaled margins, and its $\operatorname{Proj}$ is the toric
< variety of the polytope. Its degree-one basis is the sample space of Fisher's exact test, and the basic moves that
< connect it are integer circulations. The circulations thus appear three times: as the ideal of the gluing algebra, as
< the tangent directions of the coupling polytope, and as the Markov moves of exact conditional inference.
